15. Troubleshooting Common Problems
"Your branch is ahead of origin/main by N commits." You have local commits that aren't pushed. Run git push.
"Your branch is behind origin/main." The remote has new commits. Run git pull.
"Updates were rejected because the remote contains work that you do not have." Someone pushed before you. Run git pull (or git pull --rebase), resolve any conflicts, then push.
"I committed to the wrong branch."
git reset HEAD~1 --soft          # Undo the commit, keep changes staged
git stash                         # Save them
git switch correct-branch
git stash pop
git commit -m "..."

"I committed secrets by accident." Rotate the secret immediately. Then clean history with git filter-repo and force-push. Treat the secret as compromised regardless of how quickly you clean.
"I made changes in a detached HEAD state."
git switch -c new-branch-name    # Preserve your work on a new branch

"Merge conflict scared me, I want out."
git merge --abort        # or  git rebase --abort

"I deleted a branch with unmerged work."
git reflog                       # Find the last commit on that branch
git switch -c recovered-branch <commit-hash>

"git pull is creating ugly merge commits on main."
git config --global pull.rebase true    # or use --rebase on pull

"I want to see what changed in a file over time."
git log -p <file>


Final Thoughts
You don't need to memorize every command. What you do need:
A rock-solid mental model of working dir → staging → local repo → remote.
Fluency with the daily 10 — status, add, commit, push, pull, switch, log, diff, merge, stash.
Awareness of the safety tools — reflog, revert, stash, force-with-lease — so mistakes are recoverable.
One workflow picked and followed — GitHub Flow is the right choice until you've outgrown it.
gh installed — once you use it, you won't go back.
Run git status often. Commit small and early. Push before you leave for the day. Read the error messages — Git's error messages are surprisingly good at telling you exactly what to do next.
Everything else is detail you'll pick up by doing.

